a guide and they sketch each day on paper as the plan takes shape: the places, the order, the weather, the public holiday. Change something in the chat and the page is redrawn while you watch. Map and 3D in one studio03The itinerary is a map, not a listEvery stop sits on a real map and in a 3D route you can orbit, with photos, what is nearby, and the forecast or the season’s typical weather for your dates. Suggested first, then yours to accept04A budget you can see, not guessSet a target in the brief, or just say it. The budget planner suggests typical costs from traveler reports, row by row, and nothing counts toward your total until you accept it. While it plansYour guide says what it is doing as it goes: researching stops, reading what travelers recommend, checking the weather and public holidays for your dates, then writing the days. The draft streams in, and the route draws itself alongside. WeatherA forecast near your dates, the month’s typical beyond Traveler tipsLinked to the post they came from tripverse · kyoto, 3 days YouPlan 3 days in Kyoto from Delhi. I love food and old temples. TripVerseI’ve put together a balanced, three-day itinerary for your trip to Kyoto. Since you’re traveling in mid-October, you’ll be there during the pleasant autumn season, though October is part of the rainy season, so I’ve built in some flexibility. Read your brief3 days · 2 travelers · food, temples Researched possible stopsKyoto, from Delhi Checked your datesTypical for October · Sports Day on day 1 Writing your day-by-day draftDay 2 of 3 Drawing the sketchbookQueued Day Planned Date Places D1 Fushimi Inari Taisha, Takashimaya food hall Mon 12 Oct, holiday 2 D2 Arashiyama Bamboo Grove, Kiyomizu-dera, Nishiki Market Tue 13 Oct 8 D3 Okochi-sanso Villa, Nishijin, Uji Wed 14 Oct 4 Places to start Explore destinations KyotoUKY Temple districts, kaiseki, autumn maples 4 nightsJapan PortoOPO Douro riverfront, tiled facades, port cellars 3 nightsPortugal PraguePRG Old Town rooftops

